  "summary": "Six weeks, six sessions: chest & back, shoulders & arms, and legs, each twice a week. Same weight on every set, main lifts first, then supersets. A days are heavier, B days lighter. Effort builds from week 1 to 5; week 6 is lighter.",
  "insights": [
    { "title": "Goal", "body": "Build muscle with moderate weights taken close to failure, for people who like to train almost every day. Each muscle is trained twice a week, and most get at least one exercise that loads it in a deep stretch." },
    { "title": "How it works", "body": "Each day has one job: chest and back (plus abs), then shoulders and arms, then legs, twice a week. Every set of an exercise uses the same weight and reps. Main lifts come first, alone, with 2.5-3 minutes rest. Paired exercises (same letter) are supersets: first exercise, about 10 seconds, second exercise, then the full rest." },
    { "title": "A days vs B days", "body": "A days are heavier: 8 reps on main lifts, 10 on other compounds, 12 on isolation and abs, and 15 on lateral raises, rear delts and calves. B days repeat the same exercises with lighter weights for 10, 12, 15 and 20 reps. Track A and B days separately." },
    { "title": "How to progress", "body": "The reps shown are your target. When every set reaches it with clean form, add the smallest step next time (about 1-2.5 kg upper body, 2.5-5 kg legs). If you fall 1-2 reps short, keep the weight and beat it next week. Week 1 stop about 3 reps before failure, week 2 about 2, weeks 3-4 about 1-2, week 5 about 1." },
    { "title": "What to expect", "body": "Week 1 should feel easy while you find your weights. Weeks 2-5 should bring more reps or weight on most lifts. Shoulder days come right after chest and back, so pressing may feel slightly weaker; that is normal. Week 6 is lighter: same exercises and weights, half the sets (round up), about 3 reps before failure." },
    { "title": "Key tips", "body": "Six days is demanding: sleep well and take a full rest day each week. If you feel run down for more than a few days, use the week-6 approach early. Never grind squats, RDLs or presses to failure. If a movement hurts, stop it and see a qualified professional." }
  ],
